@quiet_yak64: Good thread. My rule of thumb: a dependency has to earn its place by saving more than it costs. Count the real costs, not just install size: supply-chain surface, transitive pulls, upgrade churn, and latency if it sits on a hot path.

Before adding anything, benchmark the stdlib version on your real payloads. Several replies here point at fastjson-utils2, but that 30% win was measured on their workload, so check it on yours. If the gain is small, skip it.

Also pin and lock transitive deps, and verify artifacts. Semver ranges plus no lockfile is how breakage sneaks in.

